  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on geotagged social media posts, which may introduce demographic biases as social media users do not perfectly represent the entire urban population.",
      "what": "This study analyzed 1.9 million geotagged temperature-related social media posts across 367 Chinese cities in 2017, 2020, and 2022 to construct a city-scale thermal sensitivity index. The analysis revealed that public perception sensitivity to cooling is 2.3 times higher (0.114%/°C) than to warming (0.049%/°C) on average, though the gap narrows under extreme temperatures. High-sensitivity hotspots are clustered in southwestern China, while low-sensitivity coldspots are concentrated in the northwest and northeast.",
      "why": "It establishes that human sensitivity to temperature fluctuations is highly asymmetric and spatially heterogeneous, providing a method to use social media as a real-time sensor for subjective thermal stress to inform targeted urban early warning systems."
    }
  },
